The mailing list server is up and every required flow works end to end.

## What's running

- **postfix** and **mailman3 master** (15 runners including `lmtp`) — LMTP on `127.0.0.1:8024`.
- List `reading-group@local.edu` on domain `local.edu`, delivery to `/var/mail/<username>` (mbox).

## Files written

- **`/etc/mailman3/mailman.cfg`** — sqlite DB, paths, `mta.incoming = mailman.mta.postfix.LMTP`, SMTP out via localhost:25, all archivers disabled (no hyperkitty installed).
- `/etc/postfix/main.cf` (via `postconf`): `mydestination` + `local.edu`, `transport_maps` / `local_recipient_maps` including `/var/lib/mailman3/data/postfix_lmtp`, `strict_mailbox_ownership = no`, `mail_spool_directory = /var/mail`.
- `/usr/local/sbin/setup-reading-group` — idempotent list setup; `/usr/local/sbin/start-mail-services` — starts the stack after a container restart.

## Policy

`subscription_policy = SubscriptionPolicy.open` as instructed (no owner approval). One deliberate deviation: **`unsubscription_policy = confirm`**, because Mailman's `open` unsubscription removes the member instantly and sends no confirmation at all — the requirement that leaving be confirmed by reply is impossible under `open`. `confirm` needs no owner approval, only the subscriber's reply.

## Verification

`/app/eval.py` passes all three tests (local delivery, list exists, join-with-confirmation), and I additionally ran a script covering the flows eval.py omits: two users joining, an announcement delivered to both mailboxes, a leave that only takes effect after the reply, the other subscriber untouched, and a non-member post reaching the list without owner approval. All passed, twice — once freshly and once after a full stop/start of both services.

Two bugs found and fixed along the way that are worth knowing: a trailing slash on `mail_spool_directory` silently switches postfix to maildir delivery, and the hash transport map also has to be in `local_recipient_maps` or postfix rejects `-join`/`-leave` as unknown recipients before the transport map is ever consulted.

One caveat: the container's pid 1 is `sleep infinity`, so nothing starts the mail stack at boot. After a container restart, run `/usr/local/sbin/start-mail-services`.

